end the `101 Switching Protocols` header first and then hijack the connection, so they got an error.\n\n**The change, in `response_writer.go`:**\n- `Hijack` now returns an error only when `w.size > 0`, meaning some body data has been written.\n- It still works before anything is written, and now also after only the headers are written.\n- I added a comment on `Hijack` explaining why, and changed the error message to `\"gin: response body already written\"`.\n\n**Tests, in `response_writer_test.go`:** I added a case to `TestResponseWriterHijackAfterWrite` that writes a 101 status with `WriteHeaderNow()` and then checks that hijacking succeeds. The existing case where hijacking after a body write fails still passes. `go test ./...` passes and `gofmt` reports no issues.\n\nThe changes are on a new branch, `fix/hijack-after-headers`, but not committed. I didn't want to set a git identity for you. Once you've set one with `git config user.name` and `git config user.email`, I can commit with the message I drafted, `fix(response): refine hijack behavior for response lifecycle`.","ttft_ms":4876,"type":"result","duration_ms":27457,"uuid":"dd2486dc-d105-4348-a3b4-c4ebb828d0eb","ttft_stream_ms":1814,"time_to_request_ms":183,"first_content_frame_ms":1814,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
